Math Lab Local Lab Facilitator Handbook
Refer to this document when introducing what math lab does & its structure: 
https://docs.google.com/document/d/1NbtnJBemMVCMQk3IoIj0bbAuFh2yq9XgBDlZZpMfZkY/edit?tab=t.0
Goal of this handbook: Helps Local Lab facilitators establish a local presence, find a host location, attract participants, and successfully run their first sessions.
End goal: A new facilitator should be able to identify a host, build an initial contact list, promote the first session, and run it using the handbook and its templates.


**below includes everything that the facilitator packet should include. The packet will be later found on our websites and sent to all local lab leaders to help them run sessions.
1. Introduction to Math Lab
* Explain Math Lab's mission and the purpose of Local Labs.
* Introduce our tagline: One Investigation → Multiple Levels → Shared Discovery.
* Explain how Local Labs connect to the broader network while maintaining flexibility in their activities.
2. Finding a Host Location
Explain how facilitators can find a suitable place to run sessions.
* Potential hosts: Public libraries, schools, community centers, and other local organizations.
* How to reach out: Identify the appropriate contact, send a concise introduction to Math Lab, explain the proposed activities and benefits to the community, and ask whether they would be willing to host sessions.
* What to clarify: Available space, scheduling, supervision requirements, permission to use the location, and any relevant policies.
* If the first option doesn't work: Contact other local organizations rather than relying on one potential host.
Important: Include a sample outreach email that facilitators can customize when contacting a library, school, or community organization.
3. Building an Audience Before Launch
Emphasize that facilitators should begin building interest before their first session rather than waiting until everything is ready.
Recommended timeline:
About one week before announcing the first session:
* Create a simple Google Form asking whether people are interested in attending future Math Lab sessions.
* Collect email addresses and, optionally, general availability and age group.
* Use the responses to begin building a local contact list for future announcements.
When the first session is ready to announce:
* Create flyers with the session details and a QR code linking to the registration or interest form.
* Distribute flyers around town, subject to permission from the locations.
* Post announcements in relevant local Facebook groups, following their posting rules.
* Ask local libraries, schools, town organizations, and community centers to share the announcement.
* Reach out to local weekly community emails, newsletters, and bulletin boards.
* Ask the host organization to promote the session through its own communication channels.
Explain that facilitators should use multiple outreach methods instead of relying on a single post or announcement.
Note: Curriculum Support
Local Lab facilitators do not have to develop their own curriculum from scratch. If a facilitator would prefer to use ready-made materials, they can request Math Lab to develop curriculum for their Local Lab by completing the Curriculum Request Form.


4. Promoting and Organizing the First Session
* Explain what information to include in announcements: what Math Lab is, who can attend, the date, time, location and registration instructions.
* Provide a sample flyer and announcement template.
* Explain how to manage sign-ups and send reminders.
* Recommend maintaining an email list, with consent, so future sessions can be promoted without starting outreach from scratch.
5. Facilitator Responsibilities
Explain what facilitators should do before, during, and after each session:
* Coordinate with the host location.
* Prepare investigations and materials.
* Guide mathematical exploration and discussion.
* Record attendance and session reflections.
* Communicate future opportunities to interested participants.
6. Running a Math Lab Session
* Provide a step-by-step guide to planning and running a session.
   * Leader should arrive 30 minutes prior to starting time to prepare
* Include a sample session timeline.
* Include example questions facilitators can ask.
IMPORTANT NOTE
* Please make sure to emphasize in the guide that the instructor may teach any topic relating to mathematics that they wish to the audience. The audience can be any age level K-12. However, they should spend at least a few sessions per month talking about the month’s national investigation, and should submit a solution as a group to our website.


7. The Five Levels of Exploration
* Explain how one investigation can be explored at five levels of mathematical depth.
* Describe each level and include one example investigation across all five.
* Clarify that levels represent depth, not fixed grade levels.
8. National Investigations
* Explain the monthly National Investigation and how Local Labs can participate.
* Describe how facilitators can share discoveries and feedback with the network.
* State that participation is optional and Local Labs can run their own activities.
